WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.181 --> 00:00:12.959
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:20.272 --> 00:00:23.794
One of the more interesting
aspects of the futures market is

3
00:00:23.794 --> 00:00:27.500
how exchanges create
a commoditized contract.

4
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.784
Take wood for example, you might go to
a hardware store to buy wood for shelving,

5
00:00:31.784 --> 00:00:34.934
have a look at what they have in stock and
find their supply,

6
00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.930
fall short of the needs of your project.

7
00:00:37.930 --> 00:00:43.140
In the commodity markets, the exchange
standardizes the requirements for product.

8
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.245
For example, lumber is of a specific
quality type in length and

9
00:00:47.245 --> 00:00:51.275
oil has to be of a certain
blend quality and grade.

10
00:00:51.275 --> 00:00:56.107
The advantage is that when investors are
trading contracts with the same underlying

11
00:00:56.107 --> 00:01:00.733
qualities and characteristics, it doesn't
matter who the buyer or seller is,

12
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:04.222
since everyone is trading
the same product.

13
00:01:04.222 --> 00:01:08.713
The exchange also defines when contracts
start trading, what hours they can be

14
00:01:08.713 --> 00:01:12.735
traded, and importantly,
when the life of the contract expires, and

15
00:01:12.735 --> 00:01:16.642
what happens where and when,
in the settlement and delivery chain.

16
00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.405
Buyers and

17
00:01:18.405 --> 00:01:23.830
sellers generally know that the contract
they're trading has a specific lifecycle.

18
00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.990
And in most cases the contract
is not held until expiration.

19
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.525
Rather, producers and

20
00:01:29.525 --> 00:01:34.540
end users are the ones who tend to
take delivery and deliver products.

21
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:39.000
Everyone else simply migrates to
the next actively traded contract month.

22
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.928
By creating the product and the venue,
the exchange can provide a marketplace for

23
00:01:43.928 --> 00:01:47.870
commodities and products that
few investors actually ever see.

24
00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.090
Let's take a look at an example of what a
standardized futures contract looks like.

25
00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.853
Here's an example of a NYMEX
traded crude oil contract,

26
00:01:57.853 --> 00:02:00.691
that is designed to cover 1000 barrels or

27
00:02:00.691 --> 00:02:06.550
42,000 gallons of what refiners
greatest delight, sweet crude oil.

28
00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.450
Trading months, delivery dates and

29
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.780
locations are detailed in
the contract specifications.

30
00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.851
And investors are also made
aware of delivery windows,

31
00:02:14.851 --> 00:02:18.185
settlement dates and
other crucial pieces of information,

32
00:02:18.185 --> 00:02:23.670
including when they could be physically
delivered if still holding the contract.

33
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:28.460
Remember the futures exchange facilitates
trading the highly detailed and

34
00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:33.248
commoditized product and as a central
counterpart to transactions removes

35
00:02:33.248 --> 00:02:35.717
uncertainty on the delivery process,

36
00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:39.605
as well as provides a guaranty
of settlement to all traders who

37
00:02:39.605 --> 00:02:45.340
in turn are required to post margin,
which is the subject of our next video.

38
00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.520
Come back and join us.

39
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.339
[MUSIC]